<p>I dunno, I think this one could be fixed just by finding a more creative restraining method, probably something psychological.</p>
<p>Have him befriend someone/something the next time he escapes, then hold that thing hostage far, far away. Only give him phone calls if he cooperates. Hurt it if he doesn't.</p>
<p>He appears to feel remorse for what happened to his mother. I think he's capable of caring about others (who aren't trying to kill/imprison him) enough that he'd cooperate to ensure their safety, and then it's just a matter of containing the hostage(s) and keeping this guy far enough away that he won't have enough time to rescue them if something goes wrong.</p>

